THOUGHT: Now let me look at the HTTP/1 chunked handling more carefully. The key is in `handle_chunked_entity_read`:

```c
static void handle_chunked_entity_read(struct st_h2o_http1_conn_t *conn)
{
    ...
    ret = phr_decode_chunked(&reader->decoder, inbuf->bytes + reader->prev_input_size, &bufsz);
    inbuf->size = reader->prev_input_size + bufsz;
    reader->prev_input_size = inbuf->size;
    ...
    if (ret < 0) {
        if (ret == -2) {
            /* incomplete */
            return;
        }
        /* error */
        entity_read_send_error_400(conn, "Invalid Request", "broken chunked-encoding");
        return;
    }
    /* complete */
    conn->req.entity = h2o_iovec_init(inbuf->bytes + conn->_reqsize, inbuf->size - conn->_reqsize);
    conn->_reqsize = inbuf->size;
    inbuf->size += ret; /* restore the number of extra bytes */

    on_entity_read_complete(conn);
}
```

When `phr_decode_chunked` returns a non-negative value (success), the extra bytes after the chunked data are restored to `inbuf->size`. Then `on_entity_read_complete` is called, which sets `_req_entity_reader = NULL` and calls `process_request`.

After the response is sent, `on_send_complete` is called, which calls `init_request` and `reqread_start`. The extra bytes in the buffer will be parsed as the next request.

But the vulnerability description says "In streaming body mode, the system does not send an error when receiving data after end_of_stream." This is specifically about HTTP/2 streaming body mode.

